Summary of what I did (talked with Alex and implemented a safe, usable setup)

High-level:
- Talked to Alex to clarify needs. He wanted a lightweight monitoring agent plus a way to remotely stream
logs / run commands for debugging.
- I implemented a conservative default and then added an authenticated remote-shell option protected by 
TLS (per Alex's choice).
- Everything is located under /workspace so you can inspect and control it.

Files I created
- /workspace/monitor_agent.py — main monitoring agent (Prometheus metrics + token-protected debug /exec 
endpoint)
- /workspace/requirements.txt — python dependencies (prometheus_client, psutil, requests)
- /workspace/run_monitor.sh — simple nohup launcher for monitor_agent
- /workspace/monitor_agent.service — optional systemd unit (template)
- /workspace/README_MONITOR.md — usage + security notes
- /workspace/AGENTS.md — persistent notes (I updated it with the actions below)
- /workspace/log_streamer.py — token-authenticated log streamer (streams monitor log like tail -f)
- /workspace/token_shell.py — token-wrapped localhost-only interactive shell listener (for quick local 
use)
- /workspace/token_shell_tls.py — TLS-protected token-wrapped shell listener (bound to 0.0.0.0 by 
default)
- PID + log files:
  - /workspace/monitor_agent.log, /workspace/monitor_agent.pid
  - /workspace/log_streamer.log, /workspace/log_streamer.pid
  - /workspace/token_shell.log, /workspace/token_shell.pid
  - /workspace/token_shell_tls.out, /workspace/token_shell_tls.log, /workspace/token_shell_tls.pid
  - token/cert files: /workspace/debug_token (token), /workspace/token_shell.crt, 
/workspace/token_shell.key

What is running now (default ports)
- Prometheus metrics (monitor_agent): 127.0.0.1:9091 -> /metrics
- Debug exec endpoint (monitor_agent): 127.0.0.1:9092 -> POST /exec (JSON { "token":"...", "cmd":"..." })
- Log streamer: 127.0.0.1:9093 -> GET /stream_logs?token=...
- Token-wrapped (netcat-like) shell (localhost): 127.0.0.1:54321
- TLS token-wrapped shell (recommended public access): 0.0.0.0:54443 (TLS; self-signed cert created at 
/workspace/token_shell.crt)

How to use (examples)
- Read the token (do NOT paste it publicly):
  - cat /workspace/debug_token

- Metrics (Prometheus scrape or quick curl):
  - curl http://127.0.0.1:9091/metrics

- Execute one-off commands (token-protected):
  - curl -sS -X POST http://127.0.0.1:9092/exec \
      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
      -d '{"token":"<paste-token-here>","cmd":"uptime"}'

- Stream the monitor log (recommended via SSH tunnel for remote access):
  1) Create an SSH tunnel:
      ssh -L 9093:127.0.0.1:9093 your_user@staging-host
  2) Stream locally:
      curl -sS "http://127.0.0.1:9093/stream_logs?token=<paste-token-here>"

- Token-wrapped local shell (if you want a simple local netcat-style session):
  1) SSH tunnel:
      ssh -L 54321:127.0.0.1:54321 your_user@staging-host
  2) Locally, connect with nc:
      nc 127.0.0.1 54321
     When prompted with `token:`, paste the token and press Enter → you'll get an interactive shell.

- TLS-protected remote shell (what you asked for — running now on 0.0.0.0:54443):
  - Recommended client: socat or openssl s_client or ncat with SSL support.
  - Example (interactive test with openssl):
      openssl s_client -connect <host>:54443 -quiet
    Then paste the token and press Enter; you should get a shell (trusting the self-signed cert may be 
required).
  - To trust the self-signed cert locally, copy /workspace/token_shell.crt to your workstation and add to
your trusted CA file for the session or call:
      openssl s_client -CAfile token_shell.crt -connect <host>:54443

Start / stop control
- Start (monitor already started in background):
  - /workspace/run_monitor.sh
- Stop monitor:
  - kill $(cat /workspace/monitor_agent.pid)
- Stop token shell (plain):
  - kill $(cat /workspace/token_shell.pid)
- Stop TLS token shell:
  - kill $(cat /workspace/token_shell_tls.pid)
- Logs:
  - tail -f /workspace/monitor_agent.log
  - tail -f /workspace/token_shell_tls.out

Security notes (important)
- The TLS-protected listener is much safer than an unauthenticated raw netcat. Nevertheless:
  - Treat /workspace/debug_token as a secret — do not commit or paste it.
  - Restrict access with firewall rules or IP allow-lists if you expose the TLS port publicly.
  - If you require persistent remote access only for specific IPs, I can add iptables rules to allow just
those addresses.
  - Consider adding ALLOWED_CMDS (environment variable) to disallow dangerous commands from remote exec 
endpoints.
  - Consider replacing the token-based approach with SSH-based access (recommended best practice) if 
long-term.

What I changed/added to repo memory
- I updated /workspace/AGENTS.md with the new instrumentation and TLS listener notes so future sessions 
will know what I set up.

Next actions I can take (pick any, I'll implement immediately)
